<settings version="2">
    <!-- Kodi's own "update library on startup". This is what makes the scanner
         a scanner, and it replaced a custom service addon that did the same
         thing via UpdateLibrary(video).

         The addon route looked cleaner because it needed no pre-baked
         guisettings.xml, but it never ran: Kodi discovered and registered it,
         yet CServiceAddonManager never started it, with no error logged. This
         setting is built in, does the same job, and does not depend on how
         Kodi treats an addon dropped into a fresh profile. -->
    <setting id="videolibrary.updateonstartup">true</setting>

    <!-- Do not extract thumbnails from the video files themselves.

         Default is on, and on a WebDAV source it is brutal: Kodi seeks to the
         middle of each film (gigabytes into the file), pulls enough data to
         decode a frame, and does that per title. Measured at 1-4 seconds each
         plus the transfer, against a library of thousands.

         All of that would be wasted here regardless of cost, because the
         resulting thumbnail is written to the local per-instance texture cache
         and never to the shared library. This container throws that cache away
         every run.

         Note this is NOT the same lever as <videolibrary><artworkLevel>, which
         governs what gets written to the shared library and would starve the
         streamer of artwork. This one only stops the scanner opening video
         files to make its own thumbnails. -->
    <setting id="myvideos.extractthumb">false</setting>
</settings>
